<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ndlela kaSompisi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ndlela</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ndlela kaSompisi was a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> kings Shaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>, who died in February 1840. Of non-Zulu, Ntuli origin, he distinguished himself in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe-zulu-war">Ndwandwe–Zulu War</a> and rose quickly under Shaka, who was said to promote soldiers on merit whatever their origin. Under Dingane he was the highest political and military officer of the kingdom: inDuna or chief adviser, prime minister and commander-in-chief of the army. He commanded at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blood-river">Battle of Blood River</a> in 1838, a significant Zulu defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a>. He refused to kill Dingane's half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a>, and after defeat at Maqongqo in 1840 Dingane had him executed by strangulation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ndlela, whose name means road or path, joined Shaka's army as a recruit of Ntuli origin. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe-zulu-war">Ndwandwe–Zulu War</a> he distinguished himself by his fighting and was severely wounded in battle with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a>. He was rapidly promoted and appointed chief of the Ntuli. When his promotion of non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulus</a> was questioned, Shaka is said to have replied that any man who joined the Zulu army became a Zulu, and that his advancement was a question of merit, whatever the road, ndlela, he had come by.</p><p>He kept his place at the head of the state after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> killed and succeeded Shaka, surviving under a king quick to order the execution of those he saw as enemies. A defender of traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> culture, he objected strongly when Dingane let Francis Owen of the Church Missionary Society set up a mission at the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mgungundlovu">uMgungundlovu</a>. He led an inconclusive campaign against the Northern Ndebele in 1837 and allegedly persuaded Dingane to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-retief">Piet Retief</a> and crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>. At Blood River on 16 December 1838 he commanded with Dambuza Nzobo, and the Zulu spearmen could not breach the laager of the Boers, defended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Since neither Shaka nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> had children, Ndlela, mindful of the royal line, repeatedly refused Dingane's request that he kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a>, arguing that it would diminish the king's greatness and that Mpande did not aspire to the throne. At the Battle of Maqongqo in 1840, between Dingane and Mpande, who had brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> in on his side, Dingane called Ndlela a traitor. Much of his army deserted to Mpande's general Nongalaza, and he withdrew the rest rather than be overwhelmed. Dingane then had him slowly strangled with a cowhide thong.</p><p>His failure to defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> and the rebellion against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> made him a failure in the eyes of his people, yet his inaction against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a> preserved the royal line. Mpande, who became king after Dingane, married Ndlela's daughter Msukilethe and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>; all later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> monarchs descend from Mpande. A monument to Ndlela was unveiled in KwaZulu Natal in 2004, attended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-zuma">Jacob Zuma</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ndlela kaSompisi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ndlela kaSompisi</b>, a general of Shaka and Dingane, was Dingane’s chief adviser; Ntshingwayo kaMahole commanded the army that won at Isandlwana.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
